1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,670
This video shows you how to add fields to your records from lookup tables.

2
00:00:04,720 --> 00:00:07,620
A lookup table is shared by every pipeline.

3
00:00:07,620 --> 00:00:12,402
Start with one you build by hand: which network is which site.

4
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:16,452
Name the columns network and site.

5
00:00:16,920 --> 00:00:21,984
The headquarters is 10.1.0.0/16.

6
00:00:21,984 --> 00:00:27,206
The branch office is 10.2.0.0/16.

7
00:00:27,206 --> 00:00:28,230
Save.

8
00:00:28,280 --> 00:00:33,539
Build a second table the same way: which port is which service.

9
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:43,099
Port 22 is ssh.

10
00:00:43,520 --> 00:00:49,677
443 and 8443 are https.

11
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:54,582
123 is ntp.

12
00:00:54,582 --> 00:00:56,447
Save.

13
00:00:57,680 --> 00:01:02,481
The third table comes from a file: who works on which team.

14
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:12,752
Four users, each with a team and a title.

15
00:01:12,752 --> 00:01:13,824
Save.

16
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:20,161
Back in the pipeline, add a Lookup step.

17
00:01:25,280 --> 00:01:28,783
It reads src_ip, and looks it up in the sites table.

18
00:01:28,783 --> 00:01:33,590
Its match kind is cidr: it finds the network that holds the address.

19
00:01:33,640 --> 00:01:36,044
Write the site column as a string.

20
00:01:36,044 --> 00:01:38,977
Each connection now has its site.

21
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:44,069
A second Lookup step reads dst_port,

22
00:01:44,069 --> 00:01:49,402
and matches it exactly against the port column of the services table.

23
00:01:54,280 --> 00:01:56,268
Write service as a string.

24
00:01:56,268 --> 00:01:59,773
Each connection now names its service.

25
00:02:01,760 --> 00:02:04,765
A third Lookup step adds each user's team.

26
00:02:04,765 --> 00:02:10,159
It matches user exactly against the user column of the users table.

27
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:18,945
Write team, and give it a default on miss: unknown.

28
00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:25,450
erin isn't in the table, so erin's record gets unknown.

29
00:02:27,280 --> 00:02:29,408
Save the pipeline.

30
00:02:30,640 --> 00:02:32,505
Check on learning.

31
00:02:32,705 --> 00:02:37,561
Which match kind finds the network that holds an IP address?

32
00:02:41,961 --> 00:02:42,383
cidr.

33
00:02:42,383 --> 00:02:46,694
It finds the network that holds the address.

34
00:02:47,594 --> 00:02:50,423
A record's user isn't in the table.

35
00:02:50,423 --> 00:02:52,558
What does team get?

36
00:02:56,958 --> 00:02:58,907
Its default on miss.

37
00:02:58,907 --> 00:03:01,553
Here, that's unknown.

38
00:03:02,453 --> 00:03:06,182
Next, you hold the pipeline to a schema.
